{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And the uncircumcised male</b> — Here Scripture teaches you that circumcision is in that place which is distinguishable between male and female; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 17:14). And so our Rabbis mentioned (Genesis Rabbah 46:4) other reasons as well. And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said (on Leviticus 12:3), \"his foreskin\" is well-known, for it is in the place of nakedness, and it is not so with עֲרֶלֶת לֵב (foreskin of the heart, Jeremiah 9:25) and שָׂפָה (lip, Exodus 6:12) and אֹזֶן (ear, Jeremiah 6:10), for all of them are metaphorical. In my opinion the matter is explicit in the verse, for He did not say \"and you shall circumcise your foreskin,\" which would leave the matter in doubt, and likewise He did not say \"the foreskin of your flesh\" as He says \"the foreskin of your heart\" (Deuteronomy 10:16) and the foreskin of your lips (cf. Exodus 6:12), but rather He said \"the flesh of your foreskin,\" meaning that you shall cut the flesh that is foreskin upon you — that is, the flesh that covers in you — and there is no flesh in the body that covers and blocks an organ such that cutting that flesh would leave it without foreskin, other than the flesh that covers the corona, which the Sages mentioned (Shabbat 137a). And \"uncircumcised in flesh\" (Ezekiel 44:9) is an epithet, like \"overgrown with flesh\" (Ezekiel 16:26), \"a flux from his flesh\" (Leviticus 15:2)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The word סְמוּכִים literally means \"borrowed/dependent,\" i.e., these other uses of ערלה are metaphorical, dependent on the primary literal sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:30:22.295853412Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}